WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.710
The red planet.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.270
Earth's neighbor.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:12.030
And the destination of NASA's most ambitious
mission to date.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:17.730
But this expedition will be harder than we
ever thought possible.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:24.280
It hurts to think of how hard it is, the
farthest a human being has ever been from the

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.650
earth. We've got to take every precaution.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:32.500
As NASA's astronauts arrive at Mars, they
will face a huge obstacle.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.640
Landing on the planet is a daunting task.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.500
In the past, Mars hasn't always rolled out
the welcome mat.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.840
Mars is kind of like a graveyard for
spacecraft.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.910
It's actually really hard to send something
from Earth and landed on Mars.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:59.380
This is how the European Space Agency hoped
its $250 Million Schiaparelli Lander would

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.480
touch down in 2016.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.020
But the lander systems got it wrong.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.530
The parachute, detached early, sending the
craft into freefall for 33 seconds.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:25.780
Schiaparelli smashed into the surface at 335
miles per hour, leaving a deep black scar in

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.220
the Martian landscape.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.290
It turns out that Mars is actually a
particularly difficult planet to land on.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.840
Even humanity's most brilliant engineers.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.590
We've got about a 50% success rate when it
comes to landing on Mars.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.910
The red planet is littered with dead
spacecraft that didn't nail the landing.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:53.370
And for NASA's first crewed descent to Mars,
the space agency must learn from these

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:59.160
mistakes. But as the crew hurtles towards
the surface, they're battling the same

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.560
problem as all the landers that failed
before.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:11.810
The Martian atmosphere is 100 times thinner
than Earth's, so it can't provide the drag

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.970
needed to slow a spacecraft down.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:20.520
So it's not like the Earth where you can have
these big giant parachutes that gently glide

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.840
you down to the surface.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.450
You can use some of the air, but it's hard.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.060
The Red Planet's thin atmosphere is a problem
that has been billions of years in the

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:36.720
making.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.350
Mars doesn't have a large atmosphere because
it's constantly being peeled away due to the

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.870
lack of protection of a magnetic field.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.790
The solar wind can strip away an atmosphere
on earth.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:59.210
A liquid metal core creates a magnetic field
which shields the planet and helps maintain

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:00.260
the atmosphere.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.480
But Mars is different.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.240
4.5 billion years ago, Mars and Earth formed
from dust and gas in space.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.180
Mars formed where building materials were
scarce.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.030
Its growth was stunted.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.250
So Mars is much smaller than the Earth.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.150
It's a factor of ten smaller than the Earth.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.770
And that factor of ten in mass is important.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:37.270
All of that extra mass allows the inside of
the earth to stay warm and to have a core

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.880
that's rotating, which generates a magnetic
field.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:47.600
4 billion years ago, the churning heart of
Mars started to cool and solidify.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:54.200
And with no hot core, there's no magnetic
field being generated.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:00.170
All of the high velocity charged particles
coming from the sun pick away at the

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.900
atmosphere and slowly tear it away.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.980
We know it's losing atmosphere every second
due to the solar wind.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.630
So, you know, bye bye atmosphere.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:25.900
If NASA's astronauts arrive on Mars as
planned in 2035, the settlers will find one

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.570
of the Red Planet's biggest challenges.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.210
Is it dust?

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.090
That's sticky.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.670
Basically, light from the sun can give this
stuff a static charge and then it clings to

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:44.440
stuff. So it's not just a matter of like
standing on a doormat outside your space

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.180
habitat and shaking yourself off.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.570
It's going to get in your spacesuit, it's
going to coat your visor, it's going to cover

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:50.650
your solar panels.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.220
If you get it in your lungs, it's not a good
thing.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.250
We have to figure out how to clear this
stuff out.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:04:59.620
300 feet.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:08.360
-11. Through the sixties and seventies ago,
Apollo astronauts walked on the moon.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.990
When they returned to their landing module,
they brought moon dust back with them.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:21.720
The lunar dust clogged seals, caused
equipment to overheat, resulted in false

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.380
instrument readings, and even made
astronauts sick.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:30.920
You'd want to be breathing in fine, dusty
material by itself.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.430
You can get things like silicosis.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.520
It's a it's a almost basically a lung cancer
that you can get just from breathing the dust

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.110
itself. You don't want to do that.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.610
The red planet is covered in sticky dust.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:47.430
And new research suggests it all came from
one place.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:58.670
The Medusa Fossae formation when the 600
mile long volcanic deposit formed 3 billion

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:02.870
years ago, it was around half the size of
the United States.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.060
But the Martian winds have eroded this
structure and spread the dust across the

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:09.320
entire planet.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.920
When the wind whips up this dust, it can
have disastrous consequences.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:24.110
The real problem is just that all these fine
particles get lofted into the atmosphere and

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.800
it takes a really long time for them to
settle back out.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:31.460
And what the dust does is it just gets up in
the sky and it sits there and sits there and

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:32.090
sits there.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.700
As more material gets lifted into the
atmosphere, it forms huge dust storms.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:46.040
The storms are so huge, they block out the
sunlight and cool the Martian surface.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:53.160
Creating a temperature difference between
the ground and atmosphere that causes winds

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.250
to increase and the storms to grow.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:05.240
And NASA's Opportunity Rover knows firsthand
the dangers of being trapped in one.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:15.770
Opportunity was a NASA rover sent to Mars to
hunt for signs of past water on the surface

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.140
and potentially signs of past life.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:24.730
This is a rover that we really worked with so
long, knew so well, and who had come up with

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.670
so many amazing new results from Mars.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:30.980
June 2018.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:34.310
Opportunity was 15 years into its mission.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.620
When giant clouds of dust appeared on the
horizon.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.040
Nassar could only watch as the storm grew
and grew.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:53.050
Once this dust storm coated the entire
planet, it blocked the sun, and the sun was

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.360
the source of Opportunity's power.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:00.430
Opportunity works using solar panels, and so
it gradually got less and less and less

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:01.210
electricity.

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:05.140
For opportunity, there was no escape.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.410
Lost in the darkness.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.410
Opportunities. Power reserves slowly ran
dry.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:15.970
The rover fell silent.

